A||N||6||Will||How weight distribution affects tire size||||1||
Z||000000||Will||07-05-2001||03:39 PM||||The Fiero's weight distribution is about 45/55, right?<P>In order to keep the car's traction center directly beneath its CG, a Fiero should be running 255 rear tires if it has 205 fronts.<BR>Theoretically, this might require minimal differences in front/rear spring/swaybar rates.<P>Has anyone tried this combination or some other extreme "wide rear/narrow front" tire combination? What did you think about it?<P>Does anyone who hasn't have any insights?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||07-05-2001||04:43 PM||||no scientific data behind it. i just did it cause i could. 16x7.5" wheels with 225/50-16 front and 255/50-16 rear. have to switch to coilovers for the rear. i'm pretty much thinking i'll go back to 225/45or50-16 all around next time.<P>if the choptop project goes anywhere i've got an idea to go bigger, but i still need to get to the pontiac 6000 swap before i'll know if it will work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||07-05-2001||04:54 PM||||I have 255/40/17's rear and 225/45/17's front<P>your  correct. In order to a fiero to work well , or any mid/ rear engine car for that matter you need a bigger footprint on the rear. The tire sizes I run are identical to a mid 90's 911 , and it's perfect. it dialed out a ton of understeer and made the car much more predictable.<P>I ran equal size 225/50/15's on my car for a season , it was terrible. <P>there is a point where you can get too big on the rear and it adversley affects handling. What that point is depends on the setup of your car.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||07-05-2001||05:42 PM||||Slammed, what width wheels are you running, and at what offsets?<P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-06-2001||09:15 PM||||Bump.<P>Any other takers?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||07-06-2001||09:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>I have 255/40/17's rear and 225/45/17's front<P>your  correct. In order to a fiero to work well , or any mid/ rear engine car for that matter you need a bigger footprint on the rear. The tire sizes I run are identical to a mid 90's 911 , and it's perfect. it dialed out a ton of understeer and made the car much more predictable.<P>I ran equal size 225/50/15's on my car for a season , it was terrible. <P>there is a point where you can get too big on the rear and it adversley affects handling. What that point is depends on the setup of your car.<P>JM </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How does going to a bigger rear tire dial out understeer? Or were there concurrent chassis changes you made which got rid fo the understeer but would not have worked with your previous tires?<P>My car oversteers with my new rear bar link setup. I was thinking that significantly more rear rubber would straighten that out.<P>I did a deal for a set of '98 Corvette wheels this week. I'm almost certain I can make them fit with bolt circle adapters used as wheel spacers and a bracket to move the strut inboard from the hub carrier. After that I can make caliper adapter brackets and use C5 brakes all around.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||07-06-2001||09:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> <P>I did a deal for a set of '98 Corvette wheels this week. I'm almost certain I can make them fit with bolt circle adapters used as wheel spacers and a bracket to move the strut inboard from the hub carrier. After that I can make caliper adapter brackets and use C5 brakes all around.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>remember that hotrod mag article with the 16vualve superduty engine? they used some machined pieces that moved the struts inboard and used like 17x11-13" (forgot what exactly) wheels with 315/35-17 tires. i'd love to try that. i have two sets of 17s and a set of 18s with 10" rears (8.5" fronts) that i need to switch to the 6000 pieces to use. one set would pretty much definitly fit, but probaly stick out more than i'd want (though if it's an inch or less i'd put 'em on and be rollin'), but the other two are so close when i do measurements that until i do the swap i won't know. if i could move the struts inboard even a fraction i think it would clear and tuck for certain. with either set i could then use stock vette sizes (275/40-17 or 275/35-18) and take advantage of the sale prices they get. <P>i don't know the science of it. i just love a big wheel/tire combo.||||reg||1
